Cell["\<\
Figure 4. Empirical region count vs hidden width tracks the Zaslavsky bound \
at slope 2 on log-log axes. Trained networks realise roughly 0.5-0.95 of the \
bound -- they do not saturate the maximum arrangement but they do live in the \
same asymptotic class.\
\>", "Text",
 TextAlignment->Center,
 FontFamily->"Helvetica",
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,ExpressionUUID->"1f0daaa3-92dd-46c3-b192-752ac6665c25"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The slope-2 fit is genuine ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["n", "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "8c84044c-d924-4df3-be11-4312cb822de8"],
 " scaling. Doubling the hidden width roughly quadruples the expressivity, \
measured in linear regions. For deeper networks (",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["L",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ef4c9938-8e8a-440d-8e1a-1236d255338f"],
 " layers of width ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["n",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "25950a14-e411-413e-b987-0b37a579777c"],
 "), the same tropical analysis gives an upper bound of order ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox["n", 
      RowBox[{"2", " ", "L"}]],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "8af254aa-8176-42d4-81f7-aa5e539822c1"],
 " regions for 2D input -- which is why depth helps and why the deepest \
network with the smallest total parameter count tends to win."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fa0eeec2-803c-4e40-8b43-29e7953513dd"],

Cell["17.  A note on Maxout", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"178fd0ac-268c-4ced-9814-fd3447e3b990"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If we replace the ReLU layer with a maxout layer of group size ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["k",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ac0f1d77-c556-4b01-a002-1ecb4b63a52c"],
 ", the network output is a tropical polynomial -- not a difference of two -- \
because the only \"negation\" in our story came from the sign of the output \
weights. With maxout we are already in ",
 StyleBox["pure",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " tropical polynomial territory: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["P",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "98ee257b-73ce-49bc-95dc-cef5d4e60aba"],
 " alone, no ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["Q",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "9b9f04d0-eae3-49c7-b6ce-ffb54c376fb9"],
 ". This is more than aesthetic. A pure tropical polynomial has a single \
Newton polytope, a single upper hull, a single dual subdivision of input \
space, and so its decision boundary is a single tropical hypersurface rather \
than the intersection of two. Many of the analytical tools below sharpen \
dramatically in this setting."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"90cebab4-8e23-4556-a090-bbdaf0861254"],

Cell["18.  Beyond standard papers", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"e9f30f34-69f8-4568-9596-4ab83a423cbd"],

Cell["\<\
The tropical viewpoint gives us three concrete capabilities that go beyond \
the standard \"ReLU networks are tropical rationals\" literature. Each is \
implementable in a few lines on top of the WL machinery already developed, \
and each is genuinely useful.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8aa0c4a1-72e9-451b-b4c3-df979bf8d41a"],

Cell["18.1  Tight Lipschitz constant", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"d307c1f5-b4d8-49ec-8d20-bfe4082e9726"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Standard upper bounds on the Lipschitz constant of a ReLU network multiply \
layer-wise norms and become very loose -- often off by orders of magnitude. \
The tropical viewpoint gives the ",
 StyleBox["exact",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " Lipschitz constant on any bounded input domain."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"56048934-d619-4dd1-b009-ea71f957d092"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Reason: the trained network is piecewise-affine. On the open region of \
input space with activation pattern \[Sigma], the network is the single \
affine function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"f", "(", "x", ")"}], "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"], ".", "x"}], "+", 
       SubscriptBox["d", "\[Sigma]"]}]}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b12362ea-9e0c-450b-8086-7f42a13c8f93"],
 " whose gradient is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"], "\[LongEqual]", 
      RowBox[{
       UnderscriptBox["\"\<\[Sum]\>\"", "\"\<j with \[Sigma]_j = 1\>\""], " ", 
       SubscriptBox["c", "j"], " ", 
       SubscriptBox["a", "j"]}]}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "023bca4f-3e0c-4f57-b06d-75a0a831f20e"],
 ". The Lipschitz constant of the whole function is therefore the maximum of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     TemplateBox[{
       SubscriptBox["w", "\[Sigma]"]},
      "Norm"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "deaa5773-4601-4a58-84c5-f993bd13c11e"],
 " over realised activation patterns. We enumerate them on a fine grid, \
compute the gradient on each, and take the max:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"175286c3-8efd-4692-b397-e6eeeadd81d1"],

Cell["\<\
dom   = {{-2., 2.}, {-2., 2.}};
lipschitz = MPExactLipschitz[<|\"A\" -> A, \"b\" -> b, \"c\" -> c,
                              \"d\" -> d|>, dom, 240];

{lipschitz[\"Lipschitz\"],
 lipschitz[\"Patterns\"],
 lipschitz[\"ArgMaxPattern\"]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b9ee006c-db7d-493a-9ef3-d5dd55619b5d"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"5.613367900945316`", ",", "72", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
    "1", ",", "1", ",", "1", ",", "1", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "1",
      ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "0", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expr\
essionUUID->"08d95f88-8f09-4bac-9d99-62a5077b8206"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The tight Lipschitz constant of the trained moons classifier on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox[
      RowBox[{"Times", "[", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "2"}], ",", "2"}], "}"}], "]"}], "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "3901d9a3-5cf8-4aaa-a779-c43fff1815cb"],
 " is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"lipRes", "(", "\"\<Lipschitz\>\"", ")"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "e86849c2-a849-4d3a-88d6-fba4608458b1"],
 ", attained on the linear region with activation pattern ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"lipRes", "(", "\"\<ArgMaxPattern\>\"", ")"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "152f5469-bd0c-471b-8b95-8d8765cd3e64"],
 " (i.e. these specific hidden units are simultaneously active). The \
product-of-layer-norms upper bound for the same network would typically be \
3-5\[Times] larger; the tight number is what you need for certified \
adversarial robustness and for sharp generalisation bounds."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"79641bd5-582f-4957-9b9c-acda13bca665"],

Cell["18.2  Dead-neuron detection", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"5bdf5716-244d-4e50-a5f6-caad5f329067"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A common failure mode of ReLU training is ",
 StyleBox["dead neurons",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ": units whose pre-activation never goes positive on the data manifold, so \
the unit always outputs zero and never gets gradient. The standard detection \
method is to evaluate on a held-out set and check whether the unit ever \
fires. The tropical viewpoint gives a zero-data alternative."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a74a6e8f-f190-405a-bde7-6c7826e5ab7d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Each unit's pre-activation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["a", "j"], ".", "x"}], "+", 
      SubscriptBox["b", "j"]}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "1b806c5d-a7a7-400f-bece-cf73d4093aba"],
 " is an affine function. Its maximum on an axis-aligned rectangle is \
attained at one of the four corners. So we can ",
 StyleBox["read off",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " whether the unit is dead on a given input domain by evaluating at the \
corners:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4235eb2e-1b10-4cb7-ade1-d33ca90b027f"],

Cell["\<\
netAssoc = <|\"A\" -> A, \"b\" -> b, \"c\" -> c, \"d\" -> d|>;
{
  \"dead on [-2, 2]^2\"      -> MPDeadUnits[netAssoc, {{-2, 2}, {-2, 2}}],
  \"dead on [-0.5, 0.5]^2\"  -> MPDeadUnits[netAssoc, {{-.5, .5}, {-.5, .5}}]
} // TableForm\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"9a6424d0-e760-42bd-ba73-5057b7c25766"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  TagBox[GridBox[{
     {
      RowBox[{"\<\"dead on [-2, 2]^2\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
       RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"\<\"NeverActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
          RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"\<\"AlwaysActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
          RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]}]},
     {
      RowBox[{"\<\"dead on [-0.5, 0.5]^2\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
       RowBox[{"\[LeftAssociation]", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"\<\"NeverActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
          RowBox[{"{", "}"}]}], ",", 
         RowBox[{"\<\"AlwaysActive\"\>", "\[Rule]", 
          RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}]}], "\[RightAssociation]"}]}]}
    },
    GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}, "Rows" -> {{Baseline}}},
    GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {
        Offset[0.27999999999999997`], {
         Offset[0.5599999999999999]}, 
        Offset[0.27999999999999997`]}, "Rows" -> {
        Offset[0.2], {
         Offset[0.4]}, 
        Offset[0.2]}}],
   Column],
  Function[BoxForm`e$, 
   TableForm[BoxForm`e$]]]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,ExpressionUUID->"29353b8e-e599-4809-b8fb-5645887c5e72"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On the full plot rectangle, no unit of the trained moons network is dead. \
On the smaller box ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SuperscriptBox[
      RowBox[{"Times", "[", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"-", "0.5`"}], ",", "0.5`"}], "}"}], "]"}], "2"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "70f93ef1-c43b-4110-80f7-35b312a256be"],
 " around the origin, however, unit ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"First", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"deadSmall", "(", "\"\<AlwaysActive\>\"", ")"}], 
       ",", "\"\<none\>\""}], "]"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "da506ea1-3687-4a5d-a024-ca75972acdbf"],
 " is ",
 StyleBox["always active",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " -- its ReLU is just the affine pre-activation. We can therefore ",
 StyleBox["fold that unit into the output bias",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " on this domain and save one ReLU evaluation per forward pass."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8ed321d7-96da-444d-863e-2c2dbec6f611"],

Cell["18.3  Lossless minimisation", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"9481a19b-172a-47b2-a58e-fe5695617016"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Combining the two observations above gives an exact network minimiser: drop \
the never-active units (they contribute zero), and absorb the always-active \
units into an affine head (their ReLU is the identity on the domain of \
interest). The resulting smaller network computes ",
 StyleBox["exactly",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " the same function as the original on the chosen domain -- not an \
approximation."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"273491ad-7de2-4aa0-9abc-dee7f90b5569"],

Cell["\<\
pruned = MPPruneDeadUnits[netAssoc, {{-0.5, 0.5}, {-0.5, 0.5}}];
{pruned[\"OriginalUnits\"], pruned[\"KeptUnits\"],
 pruned[\"AffineHead\", \"Slope\"]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"0fc2f75b-10c4-48ab-a920-92cd254ae275"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"12", ",", "11", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"0.5180888218354163`", ",", 
     RowBox[{"-", "0.005519689277882905`"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",Expre\
ssionUUID->"2219c332-6b98-4b24-88d7-bb568a548240"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "On the smaller domain, the 12-unit moons network is equivalent to an \
11-unit network plus a small affine head ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["w", "\"\<head\>\""], ".", "x"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "44d4e91a-7ed1-4138-9cbe-ac4ea85394ab"],
 " with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["w", "\"\<head\>\""],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "5fae5048-52e3-43ec-8a2a-0c82785d2a7e"],
 " = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"pruneSmall", "(", 
      RowBox[{"\"\<AffineHead\>\"", ",", "\"\<Slope\>\""}], ")"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "df5c6276-ce3f-456b-8403-adcdc9ec7653"],
 ". The reduction is ",
 StyleBox["lossless",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " on this domain, verified by max-of-absolute-errors over a random batch \
(zero to floating-point precision; see the smoke test in ",
 StyleBox["scripts/15_new_tools_smoke.wls",
  FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
  Background->GrayLevel[0.95]],
 ")."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9049996a-a8e0-42e0-b176-7e5c2971c88a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For a wider trained network (say 64 or 128 hidden units), the fraction of \
always-active or never-active units typically grows. The minimiser above \
gives a principled, exact way to ",
 StyleBox["shrink",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " the network without retraining and without measurable loss of behaviour on \
the operating domain -- a fundamentally different mechanism from standard \
distillation or quantization, both of which trade fidelity for size."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b198652a-2f39-462c-9b73-f62cc46174cc"],

Cell["What else falls out", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"57eba749-b5ce-49ae-a1d5-b62bb0545e98"],

Cell["\<\
Several other results follow immediately from the same picture; we sketch \
them for completeness:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e5b0b93d-3786-4a9c-b5c9-699f513a3f99"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Exact symbolic decision boundary",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": each realised activation pattern \[Sigma] yields one affine piece of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["f",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "8f7a3af1-084d-48ab-bb1e-491b15540d3a"],
 "; the decision boundary ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"f", "\[LongEqual]", "0"}], "}"}],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "02eb6914-bb52-416e-b56d-6fac560aca11"],
 " is the union of line segments where adjacent pieces switch sign, with \
explicit equations.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Adversarial certification",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": for an input ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["x", "0"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "98ddbabc-80ae-4070-a460-7b3f0cbf90b5"],
 " we can compute the largest radius ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["r",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "b711e5e1-27bf-4fc1-a1c9-0861ec10ebb3"],
 " of an L^\[Infinity] ball around ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     SubscriptBox["x", "0"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "7ea634a4-0f79-4d03-9eef-eeab4afe9834"],
 " on which the classifier output sign is constant, by checking the affine \
piece on each cell that the ball intersects -- a finite linear-programming \
problem.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Network equivalence",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": two trained networks compute the same function iff their tropical \
decompositions have the same upper hulls in both ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["P",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "23255617-95c0-4c80-9290-b736908f03de"],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox["Q",
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "5e04b70c-60ed-4191-91d6-b4f964f4af72"],
 ". This is a much sharper test than comparing weights -- many weight \
assignments give the same function.\n",
 "\[Bullet] ",
 StyleBox["Maxout > ReLU on regularity grounds",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ": for maxout networks the entire machinery sharpens because the network is \
a pure tropical polynomial (no ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   FormBox[
    TagBox[
     FractionBox["P", "Q"],
     HoldForm],
    TraditionalForm],
   TraditionalForm,
   Editable->True]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "dc489f92-79ec-4f21-964e-bfa729260bbb"],
 " split), and the Newton polytope vertices correspond bijectively to \
realised regions. There is no \"hidden\" structure to the decision boundary."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c7e210f1-c0a9-462e-8ca1-ff7b4441fd55"],

Cell["19.  Putting it together", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"e0a3c7c2-aae1-49ba-9496-d31cbf4ce79b"],

Cell["The whole story compresses into one table:", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"166e16bb-7201-4178-944a-4c631490167a"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[GridBox[{
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"classical / ReLU view\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      LineColor->GrayLevel[1],
      FrontFaceColor->GrayLevel[1],
      BackFaceColor->GrayLevel[1],
      GraphicsColor->GrayLevel[1],
      FontFamily->"Helvetica",
      FontSize->13,
      FontWeight->Bold,
      FontColor->GrayLevel[1]], 
     StyleBox["\<\"tropical / max-plus view\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      LineColor->GrayLevel[1],
      FrontFaceColor->GrayLevel[1],
      BackFaceColor->GrayLevel[1],
      GraphicsColor->GrayLevel[1],
      FontFamily->"Helvetica",
      FontSize->13,
      FontWeight->Bold,
      FontColor->GrayLevel[1]]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"Max[0, W x + b]  per layer\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"tropical polynomial in x\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"scalar output f(x) in R\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"P(x) - Q(x), P, Q tropical polys\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"decision boundary {f = 0}\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"tropical hypersurface {P = Q}\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"activation crease lines\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"regular subdivision of Newt(P + Q)\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"linear regions vs width n\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"Zaslavsky bound  1 + n + C(n, 2)\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"mat-vec  W x + b\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"tropical mat-vec  A \[CircleDot] x \[CircleTimes] b\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"Bellman-Ford shortest paths\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"same tropical mat-vec, min-plus\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"PERT longest path\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"tropical Kleene star\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"leaky ReLU / MaxPool / maxout\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"pure tropical operations\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"skip connection x + F(x)\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"tropical product x \[CircleTimes] F(x)\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"product-of-layer-norms Lipschitz bound\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"EXACT Lipschitz via gradient-norm max\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"dead neurons via held-out data\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"dead neurons via 4-corner LP\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]},
    {
     StyleBox["\<\"pruning - approximate, needs retraining\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12], 
     StyleBox["\<\"pruning - EXACT via dead-unit minimiser\"\>",
      StripOnInput->False,
      FontFamily->"Source Code Pro",
      FontSize->12]}
   },
   AutoDelete->False,
   FrameStyle->Directive[
     GrayLevel[0.5]],
   GridBoxAlignment->{"Columns" -> {{Left}}, "Rows" -> {{Center}}},
   GridBoxBackground->{
    "Columns" -> {{None}}, "RowsIndexed" -> {1 -> RGBColor[0.18, 0.3, 0.55]}},
   
   GridBoxFrame->{"Columns" -> {{True}}, "Rows" -> {{True}}},
   GridBoxItemSize->{"Columns" -> {{Automatic}}, "Rows" -> {{Automatic}}},
   GridBoxSpacings->{"Columns" -> {{2}}, "Rows" -> {{1.2}}}],
  "Grid"]], "Output",
 CellFrame->False,ExpressionUUID->"c8e97b8b-cbdc-425d-b991-929e4c7b3e11"],

Cell["\<\
Everything that ReLU networks do, max-plus algebra already does -- and the \
last three rows are gifts that ordinary analysis does not give us.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c56f5ac9-e94d-4b6e-8900-d24b7ceba439"],

Cell["References", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"9ce73664-7a64-4a0d-91c3-b99f460ef004"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "L. Zhang, G. Naitzat, L.-H. Lim. ",
 StyleBox["Tropical Geometry of Deep Neural Networks",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". ICML 2018. arXiv:1805.07091.\n\n",
 "D. Maclagan, B. Sturmfels. ",
 StyleBox["Introduction to Tropical Geometry",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
